# Target C-II — AG42 refinement of the §171 orbit/detour endpoint

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Repository base:** `5ff91a6acd3c0447be127606805e4f2cf911eadd`
**State version:** `2026-09-14.audit.51`
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit; theorem-state authority unchanged
**Dependencies:** audited-working §171 and proof-only AG42 (`eta_grid=0.00042`)

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The audited §171 orbit/detour theorem used the audited §168 constant

\[
g=0.0004
\]

and obtained the safe all-Type-II floor

\[
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge C_{146}+0.00137
=1.8208835379\ldots,
\qquad
C_{146}={4\over9}(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}).
\]

The AG42 refinement proves, at proof-only scope pending independent audit,

\[
C:={T-q/2\over D}\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042-o(1).
\]

Nothing else in §171 depends on the decimal `0.0004`.  Substituting the sharpened
value and rerunning the audited one-variable endpoint argument gives the safe floor

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}
\ge C_{146}+0.0013751
>1.82088863795.
}
\tag{OD42.1}
\]

This improves the previous explicit all-Type-II floor by `0.0000051`.  It is a real
quantitative strengthening but does not approach the separate `1.8585`
endpoint/common-cap threshold.

## 1. Reuse of the audited §171 scalar reduction

Audited §171 shows that any Type-II subsequential limit with

\[
t=\lim{q-k\over D},
\qquad
\delta=\lim{b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}\ge0
\]

must satisfy

\[
F_t(\delta):=
10t-5\pi-5\delta
+\bigl(81(\pi+\delta)^2\delta\bigr)^{1/3}
-{8\Delta_2\over {9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta}
\ge0.
\tag{OD42.2}
\]

Moreover, if the train gap is `g`, its feasible detour interval is

\[
0\le\delta\le
{ {9\over2}(t-C_{146})-g\over2}.
\tag{OD42.3}
\]

The derivative in `t` is positive throughout the domain, so for a proposed endpoint
`t_0` it is enough to show that `F_{t_0}` is increasing on its feasible detour interval
and negative at the right endpoint.

## 2. Safe endpoint with `g=0.00042`

Set

\[
g=0.00042,
\qquad
\varepsilon_0=0.0013751,
\qquad
t_0=C_{146}+\varepsilon_0.
\]

Then (OD42.3) gives the exact endpoint

\[
\delta_0
={ {9\over2}\varepsilon_0-g\over2}
=0.002883975.
\tag{OD42.4}
\]

The accompanying exact-rational certificate uses outward enclosures for `pi`,
`sqrt(3)`, and `sqrt(Delta_2)`.  It proves

\[
1.8208886379510192<t_0<1.8208886379954636,
\tag{OD42.5}
\]

and at `delta_0` bounds the three terms of (OD42.2) by

\[
10t_0-5\pi-5\delta_0<2.486503237006,
\]

\[
\bigl(81(\pi+\delta_0)^2\delta_0\bigr)^{1/3}<1.321878,
\]

\[
{8\Delta_2\over2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+g}>3.808411412392.
\]

Therefore

\[
F_{t_0}(\delta_0)<-3.0\times10^{-5}.
\tag{OD42.6}
\]

The same certificate gives a uniform lower bound

\[
\partial_\delta F_{t_0}(\delta)>142
\qquad(0<\delta\le\delta_0).
\tag{OD42.7}
\]

Thus `F_{t_0}` is increasing on the full feasible interval and its maximum is still
negative.  Since `F_t` is increasing in `t`, no feasible Type-II limit can have
`t<=t_0`.  This proves (OD42.1), conditional only on the AG42 proof-only refinement.

## 3. Numerical limit of this unchanged envelope

A high-precision regression of the same endpoint equation puts its first zero at

\[
\varepsilon_*=0.00137519051783617\ldots,
\qquad
C_{146}+\varepsilon_*=1.82088872849728\ldots .
\tag{OD42.8}
\]

The safe value `0.0013751` stays below that zero.  More decimal tuning would buy less
than `9.1e-8` in the epsilon parameter and is not a useful research direction.

## 4. Frontier effect

This refinement is global over the same Type-II scope as audited §171.  It is not the
old Section-118 endpoint/common-cap theorem, whose aggregate-detour argument already
excludes `t<=1.8585` on a narrower profile.  Therefore:

- it slightly raises the unconditional proof-only Type-II scalar floor;
- it does not close the high-`t` full-core region;
- it does not constrain the macroscopic-container branch;
- it supplies no source-preserving strict diameter decrease and no exact unit triangle.

The relevant next step is structural, not decimal: constrain the remaining high-surplus
realization of loaded-hull detour / exceptional-face perimeter, or prove that it admits
a same-cardinality strict sub-`D` competitor.

## 5. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_orbit_detour_gap_00042.py
```

The script uses `fractions.Fraction` for the sign certificate, including the cubic
upper bound and the uniform derivative lower bound.
